      <details class="wm-details" id="wm-corrections">
        <summary>Corrections</summary>
        <div class="wm-details__body">
          <p>Every correction to the map’s figures or wording since it went up on October 25, 2026, newest first. If you find an error, <a href="mailto:AndyMasley@gmail.com">email me</a>. The rules of my error bounty are on the <a href="/writing/errors-and-updates">errors and updates</a> page.</p>
          <dl>
            <dt>October 27, 2026</dt>
            <dd>Crop water now ends on the fields a satellite map (LANID) shows were irrigated in 2015, in place of the county’s acreage spread over its cropland, and the map tints that land. Industry’s, mining’s and fish farms’ water now ends at each county’s factories, mines and fish farms with an EPA discharge permit, where it has them. Where the map draws water from a river follows the new places, which changes some of the odds Follow a drop gives.</dd>
            <dt>October 26, 2026</dt>
            <dd>The data centers shown for comparison are now Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory’s projection for 2028, about 38 to 73 billion gallons consumed on site in its low and high scenarios, in place of its estimate for 2023 (17.4 billion gallons).</dd>
            <dd>The Washington Aqueduct’s water for Arlington is the county’s purchases in 2013, the latest year the cited study gives; the panel had implied 2015. The panels now say how Fairfax Water’s deliveries to each county were put together, and no longer give lengths for the Lake Gaston, Atoka and Florida Keys pipelines, which their cited sources do not state.</dd>
            <dd>The map now draws the Los Angeles Aqueduct’s water for Los Angeles from its intake on the Owens River, and the Central Arizona Project’s 2015 deliveries to Maricopa, Pinal and Pima counties from Lake Havasu. Those counties’ shares by source list that water apart from their own rivers and lakes.</dd>
            <dd>The labels and discs for the places that withdrew the most had counted reclaimed wastewater as water withdrawn. They now count withdrawals only.</dd>
            <dd>Labels and county summaries had said that crops in some California counties drew most of their water from wells. In 40 California counties USGS divides irrigation between groundwater and surface water in one statewide proportion, so the map no longer says which source those counties’ crops drew on most.</dd>
            <dd>Corrected the names of several power plants’ water sources as the USGS plant model gives them, among them Lake Keowee, the Chicago Sanitary and Ship Canal and the Intracoastal Waterway.</dd>
            <dt>October 25, 2026</dt>
            <dd>Dropped two systems the map had drawn piping water between counties without a source for how much they move or where: Cobb County’s water from Lake Allatoona and the Raritan basin system in New Jersey. Their water is back in the counties where USGS counts it.</dd>
            <dd>Removed or corrected wording the cited sources did not support: the year of a Baltimore figure, Oklahoma City’s pipeline capacity, an unsourced estimate for Houston, the Tampa Bay and Peace River figures, and notes on Puerto Rico, Minneapolis and Tulsa. Virginia’s ratio of public-supply withdrawals to home deliveries now counts the Washington Aqueduct’s water for Arlington and Falls Church.</dd>
            <dd>The national chart covers 65 years, from 1950 to 2015. Its title said 70, and it now notes that its early years leave out Hawaii in 1955.</dd>
            <dd>Lancaster County, Pennsylvania’s 2.5 billion gallons a day of power-plant water had been credited to its waste-to-energy plant. USGS counts it for Peach Bottom and Brunner Island, which stand across the Susquehanna in York County, and the map now credits it to them.</dd>
            <dd>The All-American Canal’s figure is labeled as the share diverted for the Imperial and Coachella valleys. Data centers’ electricity water uses 0.47 gallons per kilowatt-hour, matching <a href="/visuals/water">How thirsty is AI?</a>. The opening no longer implies the map shows consumption for every use.</dd>
          </dl>
        </div>
